(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["linear",{"l:linear/linked-lists-rewiring-reversal-and-dummy-heads":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A linked list is a chain of separate node objects, each holding a value and a reference to the next. Every algorithm on it is \u003cb>pointer rewiring\u003c/b>: save what you are about to overwrite, change one arrow, advance. A \u003cb>dummy head\u003c/b> (a throwaway node placed before the first) turns the head into an ordinary node, so insert, delete and merge need no special case.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost in CPython.\u003c/b> A node class with \u003ccode>__slots__ = (\"val\", \"next\")\u003c/code> takes 48 bytes per node (80 without slots), plus the value object. A Python list stores an 8-byte reference per item in one contiguous array. Linked lists lose on memory and on cache locality, because every \u003ccode>.next\u003c/code> jumps to wherever the allocator put that node. They win only when you already hold the node (or its predecessor): insert, delete and splice are then O(1) and nothing else moves. Reaching index i is O(i), so there is no binary search.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reversal invariant.\u003c/b> At every step, \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> heads the reversed part (the nodes already visited, backwards) and \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code> heads the untouched rest. The two chains are disjoint and together hold every node, so when \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code> is None, \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> is the whole reversed list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Merge invariant.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>dummy.next\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>tail\u003c/code> is the sorted merge of the nodes taken so far, and each of them is no larger than any node still waiting. Taking from \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> on ties keeps the merge stable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Doubly linked and circular.\u003c/b> A \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> pointer lets a node you hold unlink itself in O(1) without searching for its predecessor. Production code makes the list circular around one sentinel (\u003ccode>root.next\u003c/code> is the first node, \u003ccode>root.prev\u003c/code> the last), so there are no None checks at all: the Linux kernel's \u003ccode>list.h\u003c/code> and the \u003ccode>root\u003c/code> link of \u003ccode>functools.lru_cache\u003c/code> both work this way. O(1) unlink plus a dict from key to node is the LRU cache design you build in the design module.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Where singly linked lists still win.\u003c/b> Free lists, where only push and pop at one end matter: CPython's pymalloc pushes each freed block onto its pool's \u003ccode>freeblock\u003c/code> list and pops from it on the next allocation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class ListNode:\n    __slots__ = (\"val\", \"next\")\n\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = next\n\n\ndef from_list(values):\n    \"\"\"Build a list from a Python sequence; return the head (None if empty).\"\"\"\n    head = None\n    for v in reversed(values):\n        head = ListNode(v, head)\n    return head\n\n\ndef to_list(head):\n    out = []\n    while head:\n        out.append(head.val)\n        head = head.next\n    return out\n\n\ndef reverse(head):\n    \"\"\"Reverse in place and return the new head.\"\"\"\n    prev, curr = None, head\n    while curr:\n        nxt = curr.next        # 1. save the rest of the list\n        curr.next = prev       # 2. turn this arrow around\n        prev = curr            # 3. grow the reversed part\n        curr = nxt             # 4. move into the rest\n    return prev\n\n\ndef merge_two(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Merge two sorted lists by relinking their nodes (stable: ties take from a).\"\"\"\n    dummy = tail = ListNode()\n    while a and b:\n        if a.val &lt;= b.val:\n            tail.next, a = a, a.next\n        else:\n            tail.next, b = b, b.next\n        tail = tail.next\n    tail.next = a or b         # attach whatever is left\n    return dummy.next\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> is None and \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code> is the head; nothing is reversed yet\u003c/p>\u003cpre>None    [1] -&gt; [2] -&gt; [3] -&gt; [4] -&gt; None\nprev    curr\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Save the rest first: \u003ccode>nxt = curr.next\u003c/code>, so node 2 stays reachable\u003c/p>\u003cpre>None    [1] -&gt; [2] -&gt; [3] -&gt; [4] -&gt; None\nprev    curr   nxt\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Turn the arrow: \u003ccode>curr.next = prev\u003c/code>. Without \u003ccode>nxt\u003c/code>, nodes 2, 3 and 4 would now be lost\u003c/p>\u003cpre>None \u003cmark>&lt;-\u003c/mark> [1]    [2] -&gt; [3] -&gt; [4] -&gt; None\nprev    curr   nxt\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Advance: \u003ccode>prev = curr\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>curr = nxt\u003c/code>. The invariant holds again: prev heads the reversed part, curr the rest\u003c/p>\u003cpre>None &lt;- \u003cmark>[1]\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>[2]\u003c/mark> -&gt; [3] -&gt; [4] -&gt; None\n        prev   curr\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Iteration 2 makes the same four moves: node 2 now points back at node 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>None &lt;- [1] \u003cmark>&lt;-\u003c/mark> [2]    [3] -&gt; [4] -&gt; None\n               prev   curr\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Iteration 3: node 3 points back at node 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>None &lt;- [1] &lt;- [2] \u003cmark>&lt;-\u003c/mark> [3]    [4] -&gt; None\n                      prev   curr\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Iteration 4: \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code> becomes None and the loop stops; return \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code>, the new head 4 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>None &lt;- [1] &lt;- [2] &lt;- [3] \u003cmark>&lt;-\u003c/mark> [4]\n                             prev\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Writing \u003ccode>curr.next = prev\u003c/code> before saving \u003ccode>curr.next\u003c/code>: the rest of the list is unreachable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Returning \u003ccode>head\u003c/code> after a reversal: the old head is now the tail.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rebuilding a list without ending it (\u003ccode>tail.next = None\u003c/code>, or attaching the remainder): you keep stale links or create a cycle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tuple assignment assigns targets left to right. \u003ccode>curr.next, prev, curr = prev, curr, curr.next\u003c/code> is correct, but \u003ccode>prev, curr, curr.next = curr, curr.next, prev\u003c/code> writes to the \u003ci>new\u003c/i> \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code>'s next and crashes at the end of the list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Recursive reversal uses one frame per node: fine for a few hundred nodes, RecursionError for a few thousand.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Comparing nodes with \u003ccode>==\u003c/code> when you mean the same node: use \u003ccode>is\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run two iterations of \u003ccode>reverse\u003c/code> on 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5. What are \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code>, and what does each chain look like?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> is node 2 and \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code> is node 3. The reversed chain is 2 -&gt; 1 -&gt; None and the untouched chain is 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5 -&gt; None; together they still hold all five nodes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \u003ccode>merge_two\u003c/code> need no special case for an empty input or for choosing the head of the result?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The dummy is the head of the result from the start, so the first real node is attached like any other. If one list is empty the loop never runs and \u003ccode>tail.next = a or b\u003c/code> attaches the other list whole.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to rewire linked lists safely (save, flip, advance) and why a dummy or sentinel node removes edge cases.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:linear/fast-and-slow-pointers-middle-cycle-and-cycle-en":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two pointers walking the same list at different speeds, or a fixed distance apart, answer position questions in one pass with O(1) memory. When fast (two steps) falls off the end, slow (one step) is at the middle. If there is a cycle, fast must catch slow inside it. Then \u003cb>Floyd's second phase\u003c/b> finds where the cycle starts: restart one pointer at the head, step both by one, and they meet at the entry.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let \u003cb>mu\u003c/b> be the number of steps from the head to the cycle entry and \u003cb>lambda\u003c/b> the cycle length.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why they meet.\u003c/b> Slow enters the cycle after mu steps; fast is already inside. From then on fast gains exactly one node per step, so the distance from fast forward to slow (between 0 and lambda - 1) shrinks by one each step and reaches zero before slow completes a lap. They meet within mu + lambda steps: O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why the entry step works.\u003c/b> At the meeting, slow has walked t steps and fast 2t, and they stand on the same cycle node, so the extra distance t is a whole number of laps: t = k × lambda. Slow's position inside the cycle, counted from the entry, is (t - mu) mod lambda. Walking mu more steps reaches (t - mu + mu) mod lambda = t mod lambda = 0: the entry. A pointer starting at the head also reaches the entry after exactly mu steps, so stepping both one at a time they arrive together. They cannot meet earlier, because before step mu the head pointer is still outside the cycle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost.\u003c/b> O(n) time and O(1) space. A set of visited nodes is simpler and also O(n) time, but costs O(n) memory.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Gap pointers.\u003c/b> To delete the n-th node from the end in one pass, start both pointers at a dummy, move fast n + 1 steps, then move both until fast is None: slow is then just before the target, and the dummy covers deleting the head.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Beyond lists.\u003c/b> Floyd works on any function iterated over a finite set, x -&gt; f(x), because the sequence must eventually repeat. Find the Duplicate Number reads i -&gt; nums[i] as next pointers, and Pollard's rho factorisation detects its cycle the same way.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def middle(head):\n    \"\"\"Second middle for even lengths; for the first, loop while fast.next and fast.next.next.\"\"\"\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n    return slow\n\n\ndef cycle_entry(head):\n    \"\"\"Node where the cycle starts, or None if the list ends.\"\"\"\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n        if slow is fast:                 # phase 1: they met inside the cycle\n            p = head\n            while p is not slow:         # phase 2: both walk mu steps to the entry\n                p = p.next\n                slow = slow.next\n            return p\n    return None\n\n\ndef remove_nth_from_end(head, n):\n    \"\"\"Keep a gap of n + 1 nodes so slow stops just before the node to delete.\"\"\"\n    dummy = ListNode(0, head)\n    slow = fast = dummy\n    for _ in range(n + 1):\n        fast = fast.next\n    while fast:\n        slow, fast = slow.next, fast.next\n    slow.next = slow.next.next\n    return dummy.next\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">S (slow) and F (fast) start at the head. Node 6 links back to node 3, so mu = 3 and lambda = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0 -&gt; 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 6\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\nSF\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 1: slow moves one node, fast two\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0 -&gt; \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark> -&gt; \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 6\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\n     S    F\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0 -&gt; 1 -&gt; \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> -&gt; 3 -&gt; \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark> -&gt; 5 -&gt; 6\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\n          S         F\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 3: slow enters the cycle after mu = 3 steps. Going round the loop, fast (node 6) is one step behind it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0 -&gt; 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5 -&gt; \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\n               S              F\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 4: they meet at node 4. Slow walked 4, fast 8; the difference 4 is exactly one lap (lambda = 4)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0 -&gt; 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark> -&gt; 5 -&gt; 6\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\n                    SF\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Phase 2: P restarts at the head, S stays at the meeting node; both now move one node per step\u003c/p>\u003cpre>\u003cmark>0\u003c/mark> -&gt; 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark> -&gt; 5 -&gt; 6\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\nP                   S\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">k = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0 -&gt; \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark> -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark> -&gt; 6\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\n     P                   S\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">k = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0 -&gt; 1 -&gt; \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5 -&gt; \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\n          P                   S\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">k = 3 = mu: both stand on node 3, the cycle entry, exactly as the proof predicts\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0 -&gt; 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark> -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 6\n               ^              |\n               +--------------+\n               PS\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The loop guard must be \u003ccode>while fast and fast.next\u003c/code>: dropping either half crashes on an empty list or on the last node.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Even lengths: this \u003ccode>middle\u003c/code> returns the second middle (node 3 of 1, 2, 3, 4). Merge sort on a list cuts after the middle, so it needs the first middle: with the second, a two-node list splits into itself and an empty list and the recursion never ends.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Comparing values (\u003ccode>slow.val == fast.val\u003c/code>) gives false meetings when values repeat; compare nodes with \u003ccode>is\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Phase 2 restarts from the real head (not a dummy) and moves both pointers one step at a time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In Find the Duplicate Number, start at index 0: it is never a value, so it lies on the tail outside the cycle.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A list has 2 nodes before its cycle and a cycle of 5 nodes (nodes 0 to 6, node 6 links back to node 2). When do slow and fast first meet, and how many steps does phase 2 take?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>They meet after t = 5 steps, at node 5: t must be a multiple of lambda = 5 and at least mu = 2, so 5 is the first chance. Phase 2 takes mu = 2 steps (P: 0, 1, 2; S: 5, 6, 2) and returns node 2.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to find middles, gaps, cycles and cycle entries with two pointers, and why the entry step lands exactly on the entry.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:linear/stacks-for-matching-and-nesting":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Nesting closes in the reverse order it opened, which is exactly last in, first out. Keep a \u003cb>stack of open contexts\u003c/b>: push when something opens, and when something closes check that it matches the top and pop. The invariant: the stack holds every context that is open right now, innermost on top.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The Python stack is a list.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>append\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pop()\u003c/code> at the end are amortised O(1) (CPython over-allocates about 12.5%, so most appends write into spare capacity) and \u003ccode>stack[-1]\u003c/code> peeks. \u003ccode>insert(0, x)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> shift every element. \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code> works equally well; \u003ccode>queue.LifoQueue\u003c/code> adds locking for threads and is much slower. In C++ use \u003ccode>std::vector\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>std::stack\u003c/code>, in Java \u003ccode>ArrayDeque\u003c/code> rather than the legacy, synchronised \u003ccode>Stack\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why one stack is enough.\u003c/b> A closer may only close the innermost open context, which is the top. Everything deeper must stay open until the top is closed, so nothing below the top is ever needed early.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Saving context.\u003c/b> When entering a nested region changes your working state (the text decoded so far, a repeat count, a running sum), push the \u003ci>outer\u003c/i> state and start fresh; on the closer, pop and combine. Recursion does the same with the call stack; the explicit stack cannot hit Python's limit of about 1,000 frames.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Same pattern elsewhere.\u003c/b> File paths (\u003ccode>..\u003c/code> pops a directory, \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> and empty parts are skipped), HTML and XML tag matching, undo (each edit pushes the information needed to reverse it), and iterative DFS.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>PAIRS = {\")\": \"(\", \"]\": \"[\", \"}\": \"{\"}\n\n\ndef is_balanced(s):\n    stack = []                          # openers still waiting for their closer\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch in \"([{\":\n            stack.append(ch)\n        elif ch in PAIRS:\n            if not stack or stack.pop() != PAIRS[ch]:\n                return False            # wrong closer, or nothing open\n    return not stack                    # leftovers were never closed\n\n\ndef decode(s):\n    \"\"\"'3[a2[c]]' -&gt; 'accaccacc'. On '[' save the outer context; on ']' restore it.\"\"\"\n    stack = []                          # (outer parts, repeat count) per open '['\n    parts, num = [], 0\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch.isdigit():\n            num = num * 10 + int(ch)\n        elif ch == \"[\":\n            stack.append((parts, num))\n            parts, num = [], 0\n        elif ch == \"]\":\n            outer, k = stack.pop()\n            outer.append(\"\".join(parts) * k)\n            parts = outer\n        else:\n            parts.append(ch)\n    return \"\".join(parts)\n\n\ndef simplify_path(path):\n    stack = []\n    for part in path.split(\"/\"):\n        if part == \"..\":\n            if stack:\n                stack.pop()\n        elif part and part != \".\":\n            stack.append(part)\n    return \"/\" + \"/\".join(stack)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>{\u003c/code> opens a context: push it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>input   { [ ( ) ( ) ] }\n        ^\nstack   \u003cmark>{\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>[\u003c/code> opens: push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>input   { [ ( ) ( ) ] }\n          ^\nstack   { \u003cmark>[\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>(\u003c/code> opens: push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>input   { [ ( ) ( ) ] }\n            ^\nstack   { [ \u003cmark>(\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>)\u003c/code> closes: the top is \u003ccode>(\u003c/code>, a match, so pop\u003c/p>\u003cpre>input   { [ ( ) ( ) ] }\n              ^\nstack   { [\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>(\u003c/code> opens again: push\u003c/p>\u003cpre>input   { [ ( ) ( ) ] }\n                ^\nstack   { [ \u003cmark>(\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>)\u003c/code> matches the top: pop\u003c/p>\u003cpre>input   { [ ( ) ( ) ] }\n                  ^\nstack   { [\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>]\u003c/code> matches the top \u003ccode>[\u003c/code>: pop\u003c/p>\u003cpre>input   { [ ( ) ( ) ] }\n                    ^\nstack   {\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>}\u003c/code> matches the top \u003ccode>{\u003c/code>: pop. The input is finished and the stack is empty, so the string is balanced\u003c/p>\u003cpre>input   { [ ( ) ( ) ] }\n                      ^\nstack   (empty)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A stray closer with nothing open (\u003ccode>\")\"\u003c/code>): check \u003ccode>not stack\u003c/code> before popping.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Returning True at the end without checking that the stack is empty (\u003ccode>\"((\"\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Multi-digit counts: accumulate \u003ccode>num = num * 10 + digit\u003c/code> and reset after pushing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Building strings with \u003ccode>+=\u003c/code> inside nested repeats can turn quadratic; collect parts in lists and \u003ccode>join\u003c/code> once.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Trace \u003ccode>is_balanced(\"([)]\")\u003c/code>. At which character does it fail, and what is on the stack then?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At the third character, \u003ccode>)\u003c/code>. The stack is \u003ccode>( [\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>[\u003c/code> on top, which is not the partner of \u003ccode>)\u003c/code>, so it returns False. Counting each bracket type separately would wrongly accept this string; the stack catches the crossing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>decode(\"2[a3[b]]\")\u003c/code> return, and what is on the stack just after the second \u003ccode>[\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>\"abbbabbb\"\u003c/code>. After the second \u003ccode>[\u003c/code> the stack holds two saved contexts: \u003ccode>([], 2)\u003c/code> for the outer level and \u003ccode>([\"a\"], 3)\u003c/code> for the inner one, and the working parts are empty.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to handle any nesting with a stack of open contexts, saving and restoring state at each level.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:linear/evaluating-expressions-with-stacks":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Postfix\u003c/b> (reverse Polish) puts each operator after its operands, so one stack evaluates it: push numbers, and on an operator pop the right operand, then the left, and push the result. \u003cb>Infix\u003c/b> has to wait for precedence and parentheses: Dijkstra's shunting yard keeps an operator stack, and with only + and - a stack of saved (result, sign) pairs is enough.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Postfix needs no rules.\u003c/b> The order of operations is the order of the tokens. CPython compiles your code to exactly this: \u003ccode>dis\u003c/code> shows \u003ccode>a + b * c\u003c/code> as LOAD a, LOAD b, LOAD c, BINARY_OP (*), BINARY_OP (+). The interpreter is a stack machine whose \u003ccode>BINARY_OP\u003c/code> pops two values and pushes one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shunting yard in one rule.\u003c/b> Before pushing an operator, pop and apply every operator on the stack that binds at least as tightly (strictly tighter for right-associative ones such as \u003ccode>**\u003c/code>), stopping at \u003ccode>(\u003c/code>; on \u003ccode>)\u003c/code>, pop until the matching \u003ccode>(\u003c/code>. Each token is pushed and popped at most once: O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Plus, minus and parentheses\u003c/b> (Basic Calculator). Keep the running \u003ccode>result\u003c/code>, the \u003ccode>sign\u003c/code> of the next number and its digits \u003ccode>num\u003c/code>. On \u003ccode>(\u003c/code> push the outer \u003ccode>(result, sign)\u003c/code> and restart; on \u003ccode>)\u003c/code> finish the inner result, pop, and combine \u003ccode>outer + outer_sign * inner\u003c/code>. A unary minus needs no extra code: \u003ccode>-(\u003c/code> sets the sign before the push.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Times and divide without parentheses\u003c/b> (Basic Calculator II, in the bank). Keep a stack of signed terms: push \u003ccode>+n\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>-n\u003c/code>, and combine \u003ccode>*\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>/\u003c/code> with the top at once; the answer is the sum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Division.\u003c/b> Problem statements, C and Java truncate towards zero; Python's \u003ccode>//\u003c/code> floors. \u003ccode>-7 // 2\u003c/code> is -4 but truncation gives -3. \u003ccode>int(a / b)\u003c/code> truncates too, through a float that is exact only up to 2^53.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def div_trunc(a, b):\n    \"\"\"Integer division that truncates towards zero, like C and Java (Python's // floors).\"\"\"\n    q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n    return q if (a &gt;= 0) == (b &gt;= 0) else -q\n\n\ndef eval_rpn(tokens):\n    stack = []\n    for tok in tokens:\n        if tok in (\"+\", \"-\", \"*\", \"/\"):\n            b = stack.pop()             # right operand is on top\n            a = stack.pop()\n            if tok == \"+\":\n                stack.append(a + b)\n            elif tok == \"-\":\n                stack.append(a - b)\n            elif tok == \"*\":\n                stack.append(a * b)\n            else:\n                stack.append(div_trunc(a, b))\n        else:\n            stack.append(int(tok))      # handles \"-3\" as a number\n    return stack[-1]\n\n\ndef calculate(s):\n    \"\"\"+, -, parentheses, unary minus and spaces.\"\"\"\n    result, sign, num = 0, 1, 0\n    stack = []                          # (result so far, sign before '(') per open group\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch.isdigit():\n            num = num * 10 + int(ch)\n        elif ch in \"+-\":\n            result += sign * num\n            num, sign = 0, (1 if ch == \"+\" else -1)\n        elif ch == \"(\":\n            stack.append((result, sign))\n            result, sign = 0, 1\n        elif ch == \")\":\n            result += sign * num\n            num = 0\n            outer, outer_sign = stack.pop()\n            result = outer + outer_sign * result\n    return result + sign * num\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>calculate(\"8-(3-(2+4))\")\u003c/code>. Read \u003ccode>8-\u003c/code>: result = 8, and the next number gets sign -1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>8-(3-(2+4))\n ^\nresult 8   sign -1   num 0   stack []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>(\u003c/code>: save the outer state (8, -1) and start a fresh group\u003c/p>\u003cpre>8-(3-(2+4))\n  ^\nresult 0   sign +1   num 0   stack [\u003cmark>(8, -1)\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>3-\u003c/code>: inside the group, result = 3 and the next sign is -1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>8-(3-(2+4))\n    ^\nresult 3   sign -1   num 0   stack [(8, -1)]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>(\u003c/code>: save (3, -1) and start the inner group\u003c/p>\u003cpre>8-(3-(2+4))\n     ^\nresult 0   sign +1   num 0   stack [(8, -1), \u003cmark>(3, -1)\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>2+4\u003c/code>: result = 2 and num = 4 is still pending\u003c/p>\u003cpre>8-(3-(2+4))\n        ^\nresult 2   sign +1   num 4   stack [(8, -1), (3, -1)]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>)\u003c/code>: the inner group is 2 + 4 = 6; pop (3, -1) and combine: 3 - 6 = -3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>8-(3-(2+4))\n         ^\nresult \u003cmark>-3\u003c/mark>   sign +1   num 0   stack [(8, -1)]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>)\u003c/code>: pop (8, -1) and combine: 8 - (-3) = 11. Input ends with nothing pending: the answer is 11\u003c/p>\u003cpre>8-(3-(2+4))\n          ^\nresult \u003cmark>11\u003c/mark>   sign +1   num 0   stack []\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Popping operands in the wrong order: the top is the \u003ci>right\u003c/i> operand of \u003ccode>a - b\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>a / b\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treating \u003ccode>\"-3\"\u003c/code> as an operator in postfix input: test membership in the operator set, not the first character.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Forgetting the last pending number: return \u003ccode>result + sign * num\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Calling \u003ccode>eval\u003c/code> on input: it runs arbitrary code, and its \u003ccode>/\u003c/code> returns floats.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>eval_rpn([\"2\", \"1\", \"+\", \"3\", \"*\"])\u003c/code> return, and what is on the stack just before the \u003ccode>*\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>9. After \u003ccode>2 1 +\u003c/code> the stack is \u003ccode>[3]\u003c/code>; pushing \u003ccode>3\u003c/code> makes it \u003ccode>[3, 3]\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>*\u003c/code> pops both and pushes 9.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to evaluate postfix with one stack and infix with saved signs or an operator stack, without eval.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:linear/queues-deques-and-ring-buffers":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A queue adds at the back and removes at the front. A Python list does the back well and the front badly, because \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> shifts every element, so use \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code>: O(1) at both ends. When the capacity is fixed, a \u003cb>ring buffer\u003c/b> (an array, a head index and a size, wrapping with modulo) gives the same O(1) with no allocation at all.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why not a list.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>insert(0, x)\u003c/code> move every remaining reference. Draining 100,000 items from the front of a list took about 3.6 s on the machine used for this guide; \u003ccode>deque.popleft\u003c/code> took about 9 ms. A BFS written with \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> silently becomes quadratic.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>collections.deque\u003c/b> is a doubly linked list of blocks, each holding 64 references (\u003ccode>BLOCKLEN 64\u003c/code> in CPython's \u003ccode>_collectionsmodule.c\u003c/code>). Appends and pops at either end write into the end block or link a fresh one, so no element ever moves, \u003ccode>realloc\u003c/code> is never called, and the link overhead is two pointers per 64 items instead of per item. The price: \u003ccode>d[i]\u003c/code> walks blocks from the nearer end, O(n) in the middle, so use a list for random access. \u003ccode>deque(maxlen=n)\u003c/code> discards from the opposite end when full, a ready-made \"last n items\" buffer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ring buffer invariant.\u003c/b> Item i of the queue lives at \u003ccode>(head + i) % capacity\u003c/code> for i from 0 to size - 1. Keep a \u003ccode>size\u003c/code> (or leave one slot unused), because with only head and tail indices \u003ccode>head == tail\u003c/code> means both empty and full. With a power-of-two capacity, \u003ccode>% cap\u003c/code> becomes the bit mask \u003ccode>&amp; (cap - 1)\u003c/code>: the Linux kernel's \u003ccode>kfifo\u003c/code> sizes are powers of two for this reason. io_uring talks to the kernel through two ring buffers shared with user space (submission and completion queues), and network cards use descriptor rings in the same way.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Across threads and machines.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>queue.Queue\u003c/code> blocks on \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> and locks around every operation, for producer and consumer threads. Redis lists serve as job queues between processes: \u003ccode>LPUSH\u003c/code> to add, blocking \u003ccode>BRPOP\u003c/code> to take, and \u003ccode>BLMOVE\u003c/code> to move a job atomically into an \"in progress\" list so a crashed worker does not lose it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class RingBuffer:\n    \"\"\"Fixed-capacity FIFO queue in a circular array.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.buf = [None] * capacity\n        self.head = 0                   # index of the oldest item\n        self.size = 0\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        if self.size == len(self.buf):\n            raise OverflowError(\"ring buffer is full\")\n        self.buf[(self.head + self.size) % len(self.buf)] = x\n        self.size += 1\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if self.size == 0:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from an empty ring buffer\")\n        x, self.buf[self.head] = self.buf[self.head], None   # drop the reference\n        self.head = (self.head + 1) % len(self.buf)\n        self.size -= 1\n        return x\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Capacity 4. Push a, b, c: each goes to (head + size) % 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot     0    1    2    3\nbuf      a    b    c    .\nhead 0, size 3, next write at 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop twice: a, then b. Head moves forward; the slots are cleared so the objects can be freed\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot     0    1    2    3\nbuf      .    .    \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>    .\nhead 2, size 1, next write at 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Push d into slot 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot     0    1    2    3\nbuf      .    .    c    \u003cmark>d\u003c/mark>\nhead 2, size 2, next write at (2 + 2) % 4 = 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Push e: the write index wraps round to slot 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot     0    1    2    3\nbuf      \u003cmark>e\u003c/mark>    .    c    d\nhead 2, size 3, next write at 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Push f: size equals capacity, so the buffer is full; another push raises OverflowError\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot     0    1    2    3\nbuf      e    \u003cmark>f\u003c/mark>    c    d\nhead 2, size 4 (full)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Pop returns c, the oldest item, from slot 2; head wraps forward to 3. Queue order is d, e, f\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot     0    1    2    3\nbuf      e    f    .    d\nhead \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>, size 3, next write at (3 + 3) % 4 = 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code> in a BFS or a scheduler loop: O(n) per call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Not clearing a slot on pop keeps the object alive (a leak when items are large).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>deque[i]\u003c/code> inside a loop is O(n) per access in the middle; deques are for the ends.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In C and Java, \u003ccode>(head - 1) % cap\u003c/code> can be negative; Python's \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> never is for a positive capacity.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does CPython's deque use 64-item blocks instead of one node per item or one big circular array?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One node per item would cost two pointers and one allocation per element. One big array would need occasional O(n) copies when it grows (fine amortised, but every element moves). Blocks of 64 keep the per-item overhead and the number of allocations small, never move existing elements, and keep neighbours together in memory for better cache behaviour.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to pick a FIFO structure in Python and how deques and ring buffers achieve O(1) at the ends.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:linear/a-queue-from-two-stacks-and-why-it-is-amortised-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Push onto an \u003cb>in\u003c/b> stack; pop from an \u003cb>out\u003c/b> stack; only when \u003ci>out\u003c/i> is empty, move everything across, which reverses it into FIFO order. One pop can cost O(n), but each element is moved at most once in its life, so any m operations cost O(m) in total: \u003cb>amortised O(1)\u003c/b> per operation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why the order is right.\u003c/b> \u003ci>in\u003c/i> holds the newest items with the newest on top. Pouring it onto an empty \u003ci>out\u003c/i> puts the oldest on top. Never pour while \u003ci>out\u003c/i> still has items: they are older than everything in \u003ci>in\u003c/i> and must leave first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Aggregate argument.\u003c/b> Follow one element: it is pushed onto \u003ci>in\u003c/i> (1 operation), moved during one transfer (a pop and a push, 2), and popped from \u003ci>out\u003c/i> (1). That is at most 4 stack operations over its whole life, whatever the interleaving. m queue operations involve at most m elements, so the total work is at most about 4m.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Banker's argument.\u003c/b> Charge each push 3 units instead of 1 and leave 2 on the element. A transfer spends exactly the 2 units stored on each element it moves, so the bank never goes negative and every operation's charged cost is constant. (The potential function 2 × len(in) says the same thing.)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>When amortised is not enough.\u003c/b> The bound is about totals: one unlucky pop still moves n elements. Hard latency targets (audio, trading, kernel paths) prefer a ring buffer or a deque with true O(1) operations. Functional languages use this front-and-back-list queue because it works with immutable lists.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why it matters later.\u003c/b> Store \u003ccode>(x, max so far)\u003c/code> in each stack and the queue reports its maximum in O(1) amortised: one way to get every sliding-window maximum, next to the monotonic deque in the monotonic stacks module.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TwoStackQueue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.inbox.append(x)\n\n    def _refill(self):\n        if not self.outbox:             # only when empty: this keeps FIFO order\n            while self.inbox:\n                self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())\n\n    def pop(self):\n        self._refill()\n        return self.outbox.pop()        # IndexError when the queue is empty\n\n    def peek(self):\n        self._refill()\n        return self.outbox[-1]\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.inbox) + len(self.outbox)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">push 1, 2, 3: each is one append to in (top on the right)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>in   [1, 2, 3]\nout  []\nmoves so far: 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pop: out is empty, so pour in across: 3, then 2, then 1. The oldest, 1, is now on top of out and is returned\u003c/p>\u003cpre>in   []\nout  [3, 2]          returned \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nmoves so far: \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">push 4: goes to in; out still holds older items and is left alone\u003c/p>\u003cpre>in   [\u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>]\nout  [3, 2]\nmoves so far: 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pop: out is not empty, so no transfer; return 2 in O(1)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>in   [4]\nout  [3]             returned \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nmoves so far: 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pop: return 3, still no transfer\u003c/p>\u003cpre>in   [4]\nout  []              returned \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\nmoves so far: 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pop: out is empty again, so pour the single item across and return 4. Four items, four moves in total\u003c/p>\u003cpre>in   []\nout  []              returned \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\nmoves so far: \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Pouring on every pop and pouring back on every push: each operation becomes O(n), O(n^2) overall.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pouring while \u003ci>out\u003c/i> is not empty: newer items land on top of older ones and FIFO order breaks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>peek\u003c/code> must refill exactly like \u003ccode>pop\u003c/code>, and the size is \u003ccode>len(in) + len(out)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Push 1 to 5, pop, push 6, pop, pop. How many element moves happen in total, and what do the stacks hold at the end?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>5 moves, all during the first pop, which returns 1. The next two pops return 2 and 3 straight from \u003ci>out\u003c/i>. At the end \u003ci>in\u003c/i> is \u003ccode>[6]\u003c/code> and \u003ci>out\u003c/i> is \u003ccode>[5, 4]\u003c/code>, with 4 on top, the next item to leave.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to build a queue from two stacks and prove its amortised O(1) cost by counting each element's moves.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:linear/deque-three-ways-list-linked-list-and-ring-buffe":"\u003cp>You will build the same four-operation deque (\u003ccode>append\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>appendleft\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pop\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>popleft\u003c/code>) on three different foundations, check all three against \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code> with a random tester, and time them on a queue workload. If you built the \u003ccode>dsakit\u003c/code> stress runner in the correctness module, use it; the 30-line tester below does the same job.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>deque3.py\u003c/code>. Read each class before running it and predict the cost of \u003ccode>appendleft\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>popleft\u003c/code> for each.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\nimport time\nfrom collections import deque\n\n\nclass ListDeque:\n    \"\"\"Python list underneath: the right end is cheap, the left end shifts every element.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.a = []\n\n    def append(self, x):\n        self.a.append(x)\n\n    def appendleft(self, x):\n        self.a.insert(0, x)              # O(n)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if not self.a:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from an empty deque\")\n        return self.a.pop()\n\n    def popleft(self):\n        if not self.a:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from an empty deque\")\n        return self.a.pop(0)             # O(n)\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.a)\n\n\nclass _Node:\n    __slots__ = (\"val\", \"prev\", \"next\")\n\n    def __init__(self, val=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.prev = self.next = self\n\n\nclass LinkedDeque:\n    \"\"\"Circular doubly linked list with one sentinel: no None checks at either end.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = _Node()              # root.next is the front, root.prev the back\n        self.n = 0\n\n    def _insert_after(self, node, val):\n        new = _Node(val)\n        new.prev, new.next = node, node.next\n        node.next.prev = new\n        node.next = new\n        self.n += 1\n\n    def _unlink(self, node):\n        if node is self.root:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from an empty deque\")\n        node.prev.next = node.next\n        node.next.prev = node.prev\n        self.n -= 1\n        return node.val\n\n    def append(self, x):\n        self._insert_after(self.root.prev, x)\n\n    def appendleft(self, x):\n        self._insert_after(self.root, x)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self._unlink(self.root.prev)\n\n    def popleft(self):\n        return self._unlink(self.root.next)\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return self.n\n\n\nclass RingDeque:\n    \"\"\"Circular array that doubles when full; item i lives at (head + i) % capacity.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity=8):\n        self.buf = [None] * capacity\n        self.head = 0\n        self.n = 0\n\n    def _grow(self):\n        old, cap = self.buf, len(self.buf)\n        self.buf = [old[(self.head + i) % cap] for i in range(self.n)] + [None] * cap\n        self.head = 0\n\n    def append(self, x):\n        if self.n == len(self.buf):\n            self._grow()\n        self.buf[(self.head + self.n) % len(self.buf)] = x\n        self.n += 1\n\n    def appendleft(self, x):\n        if self.n == len(self.buf):\n            self._grow()\n        self.head = (self.head - 1) % len(self.buf)\n        self.buf[self.head] = x\n        self.n += 1\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if self.n == 0:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from an empty deque\")\n        i = (self.head + self.n - 1) % len(self.buf)\n        x, self.buf[i] = self.buf[i], None\n        self.n -= 1\n        return x\n\n    def popleft(self):\n        if self.n == 0:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from an empty deque\")\n        x, self.buf[self.head] = self.buf[self.head], None\n        self.head = (self.head + 1) % len(self.buf)\n        self.n -= 1\n        return x\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return self.n\n\n\nOPS = (\"append\", \"appendleft\", \"pop\", \"popleft\")\n\n\ndef stress(cls, trials=2000, steps=200, seed=0):\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    for t in range(trials):\n        mine, ref, log = cls(), deque(), []\n        for _ in range(rng.randint(0, steps)):\n            op = rng.choice(OPS)\n            log.append(op)\n            if op.startswith(\"append\"):\n                x = rng.randint(0, 99)\n                getattr(mine, op)(x)\n                getattr(ref, op)(x)\n            else:\n                want = got = \"IndexError\"\n                try:\n                    want = getattr(ref, op)()\n                except IndexError:\n                    pass\n                try:\n                    got = getattr(mine, op)()\n                except IndexError:\n                    pass\n                assert got == want, (cls.__name__, t, log)\n            assert len(mine) == len(ref), (cls.__name__, t, log)\n    print(f\"{cls.__name__}: {trials} random runs agree with collections.deque\")\n\n\ndef queue_workload(cls, n):\n    \"\"\"Fill with n items, then n rounds of append + popleft (a FIFO queue at steady size n).\"\"\"\n    d = cls()\n    for i in range(n):\n        d.append(i)\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    for i in range(n):\n        d.append(i)\n        d.popleft()\n    return time.perf_counter() - t0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    for cls in (ListDeque, LinkedDeque, RingDeque):\n        stress(cls)\n    kinds = (ListDeque, LinkedDeque, RingDeque, deque)\n    print(f\"{'n':&gt;8} \" + \" \".join(f\"{c.__name__:&gt;12}\" for c in kinds))\n    for n in (10_000, 20_000, 40_000, 80_000):\n        row = [min(queue_workload(c, n) for _ in range(3)) for c in kinds]\n        print(f\"{n:&gt;8} \" + \" \".join(f\"{t * 1e3:&gt;10.1f}ms\" for t in row))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python3 deque3.py\u003c/code>. All three stress tests must pass before you look at any timing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Break \u003ccode>LinkedDeque._insert_after\u003c/code> on purpose: swap the last two lines so \u003ccode>node.next = new\u003c/code> runs before \u003ccode>node.next.prev = new\u003c/code>. Run the stress test, read the failing operation log it prints, and explain from a drawing which link is now wrong. Restore the order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Break \u003ccode>RingDeque._grow\u003c/code> by copying \u003ccode>old\u003c/code> as it is (\u003ccode>self.buf = old + [None] * cap\u003c/code>) without unrolling from \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>. Find a failing case, then explain why it only fails after the head has wrapped.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write down, before reading the timing table, how the time per operation should change when n doubles for each column. Then compare with the table and compute the ratios.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Optional: add \u003ccode>rotate(k)\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>RingDeque\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>LinkedDeque\u003c/code>, and a stress test against \u003ccode>deque.rotate\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The three stress tests pass with the correct code, and each planted bug fails within the first few random runs. The linked-list bug fails on a log as short as \u003ccode>append, pop\u003c/code>: the sentinel's back link is never updated, so \u003ccode>pop\u003c/code> finds the sentinel and reports an empty deque; the ring bug needs the head to move and the buffer to fill first, so its shortest failing log is about ten operations long.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The list version's total time grows about four times per doubling of n once the list is large (from a few microseconds per operation at 10,000 items to about 50 at 80,000 on the machine used here), because every \u003ccode>pop(0)\u003c/code> shifts n references.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The linked and ring versions stay roughly flat at 0.5 to 2 microseconds per operation, and \u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code> at about 0.1: roughly ten times faster again, because its loop runs in C over 64-item blocks instead of Python objects per item. Exact numbers vary by machine and run; the shapes do not.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The planted \u003ccode>RingDeque\u003c/code> bug survives until an \u003ccode>appendleft\u003c/code> (or a \u003ccode>popleft\u003c/code>) has moved \u003ccode>head\u003c/code> away from 0 before a resize; while head is 0 the old array is already in order, so copying it unchanged happens to be right.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your linked and ring deques have the same O(1) bounds. Which would you expect to use less memory per item, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The ring deque: one 8-byte reference per slot (with at most half the slots empty just after a doubling), while each linked node is a separate object of about 56 bytes with \u003ccode>__slots__\u003c/code> (header plus three references) on top of its value. That memory gap is also part of why the linked version is no faster despite never copying.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","p:206":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turn a finite singly linked chain around in place and return its new head. The existing node objects must be reused: a chain with values 3, 5, 8 becomes 8, 5, 3. An empty chain has no head to return.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node has a value and one \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> reference; assume the input is acyclic. The algorithm must not rely on random access or make a second chain. A constant number of node references suffices, even for chains too long for Python recursion.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every arrow in a singly linked chain must point the other way, without building a new list.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Walk once from the head. At each node, what must you remember before you change its next pointer?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Pointer rewiring with a dummy head\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep prev (head of the part already reversed) and curr (head of the rest). Save nxt = curr.next, point curr.next at prev, then advance prev = curr and curr = nxt. When curr is None, prev is the new head.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Collect all node references in an array, then connect them in reverse order and terminate the old head. This visits and rewires n nodes in O(n) time but needs O(n) additional references. Repeatedly searching for the current tail instead uses O(1) space but takes O(n²) time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At every iteration \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> heads exactly the already reversed prefix and \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code> heads the untouched suffix. Save \u003ccode>curr.next\u003c/code> before redirecting it: otherwise that suffix would no longer be reachable. Attaching the current node to \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> increases the reversed prefix by one without losing any node.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prev = None, curr = 3 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>save 5, then point 3 to None\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prev = 3, curr = 5 -&gt; 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>save 8 and point 5 to 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prev = 5 -&gt; 3, curr = 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>reverse the last arrow; return \u003cmark>8 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The two parts start disjoint and cover the input. Each step transfers exactly one node and preserves this invariant. When the suffix is empty, the prefix contains all nodes in the desired order, so its head is the answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class ListNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = next\n\n\ndef reverse_list(head):\n    prev, curr = None, head\n    while curr is not None:\n        following = curr.next\n        curr.next = prev\n        prev, curr = curr, following\n    return prev\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is processed once: O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. The input links are modified; values and node identities are preserved.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Saving the successor after overwriting \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> loses the unprocessed suffix.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Returning the old head returns the new tail instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty and one-node lists require no special branch.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse only a bounded segment by reconnecting its two boundary links.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reverse a doubly linked list by exchanging both directions at every node.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:21":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given two sorted singly linked chains, combine them into one sorted chain by relinking their existing nodes. For 1 → 4 and 2 → 3, return 1 → 2 → 3 → 4. Equal values are allowed and each node must appear exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Inputs are finite, acyclic, nondecreasing, and disjoint; an input may be empty. Relinking avoids allocating one new node per result element. If the chains shared a node, naive rewiring could create a cycle, so disjointness is important.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two chains that are already in order must become one chain in order, reusing their nodes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Deciding which node becomes the head of the result is a special case. How can you avoid making it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Pointer rewiring with a dummy head\u003c/p>\u003cp>Start from a dummy node with a tail pointer. Repeatedly attach the smaller of the two current heads and advance that list; when one runs out, attach the other whole. Return dummy.next.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m + n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Gather all node references in an array, sort by value, and reconnect consecutive references. This costs O((m+n) log(m+n)) time and O(m+n) auxiliary space. It ignores the useful ordering already present in each chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The smallest remaining node must be one of the two heads. A sentinel before the result allows the same append operation even for the first node. Taking the left head on ties makes the merge stable with respect to the two input chains.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Heads 1 and 2; result is empty\u003c/p>\u003cpre>attach 1, advance its chain to 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Heads 4 and 2, then 4 and 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>attach 2 and 3 in that order\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The second chain is empty\u003c/p>\u003cpre>attach the entire remainder: \u003cmark>1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The output prefix is sorted and every appended node is no larger than either remaining head. Since each remainder is sorted, choosing the smaller head preserves that property. When a chain ends, the other remainder is already sorted and can be attached whole.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class ListNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = next\n\n\ndef merge_lists(a, b):\n    dummy = tail = ListNode()\n    while a is not None and b is not None:\n        if a.val &lt;= b.val:\n            tail.next, a = a, a.next\n        else:\n            tail.next, b = b, b.next\n        tail = tail.next\n    tail.next = a if a is not None else b\n    return dummy.next\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At most m+n nodes are examined, for O(m+n) time and O(1) auxiliary space including the sentinel. The input links change, but result nodes are the original nodes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return \u003ccode>dummy.next\u003c/code>, not the artificial sentinel.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Advance the selected source and the tail; forgetting either can lose nodes or loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Either or both inputs may be empty; the final attachment handles them.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Merge descending chains by reversing the comparison.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Merge several chains by maintaining a heap of their current heads.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:141":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Decide whether repeated \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> references from a given head ever revisit a node. A chain may end at None or eventually enter a loop. Two different nodes with equal values are not a cycle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node has at most one successor, and node identity, not stored value, determines repetition. Use constant extra memory rather than storing every visited reference. The head may be None or a node pointing to itself.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Decide whether following next pointers ever ends, using constant memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Picture two runners on a track, one twice as fast as the other. What happens if the track loops?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fast and slow pointers (Floyd)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Move slow one node and fast two nodes per step. If fast reaches None there is no cycle; if there is one, fast gains one node per step inside it and must land on slow within one lap. Compare nodes with \u003ccode>is\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Record each visited node identity in a set. Encountering a recorded node proves a loop; reaching None proves termination. This takes O(n) time and O(n) space for n distinct reachable nodes. It is straightforward but stores the entire path.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Move one pointer by one edge and another by two. In an acyclic chain, the faster pointer eventually runs off the end. Once both pointers are inside a cycle, their positions modulo the cycle length differ by one additional step each iteration, so the difference eventually becomes zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Chain A -&gt; B -&gt; C -&gt; B, both pointers begin at A\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slow reaches B; fast reaches C\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Move again: slow reaches C, fast follows C -&gt; B -&gt; C\u003c/p>\u003cpre>both references identify \u003cmark>C\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A meeting means the successor walk repeats\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If a meeting occurs after movement, following the same deterministic successor relation returns to the same node, so a cycle exists. Conversely, after entering a cycle the relative position advances through all residues and must meet within one lap. Thus neither outcome is a false positive.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class ListNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = next\n\n\ndef has_cycle(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast is not None and fast.next is not None:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n        if slow is fast:\n            return True\n    return False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With n distinct reachable nodes, the tail and at most one cycle lap take O(n) time. Only two references are stored, so auxiliary space is O(1); the chain is unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compare references with \u003ccode>is\u003c/code>, not node values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Test both \u003ccode>fast\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>fast.next\u003c/code> before advancing two steps.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A self-loop meets after one step; an empty input immediately returns False.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>After a meeting, find the cycle entry by restarting one pointer at the head.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Measure the cycle length by walking once around from a meeting point.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:20":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Determine whether a string containing only the three bracket pairs \u003ccode>()\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>{}\u003c/code> is properly nested and fully closed. For instance, \u003ccode>{[()]}\u003c/code> is valid but \u003ccode>([)]\u003c/code> is not. The empty string is valid.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every input character is one of the six brackets. A closing bracket must match the most recent opener that has not already been closed. Counts alone do not capture nesting order, so the relevant history must be retained.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Three kinds of brackets must close in the reverse of the order they opened.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When a closing bracket arrives, which opening bracket is the only one it may legally close?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Stack of open contexts\u003c/p>\u003cp>Push every opener. On a closer the stack must be non-empty and its top must be the matching opener, which you pop. At the end the stack must be empty, otherwise something was never closed.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repeatedly remove any adjacent matching pair until no further removal is possible; accept exactly when the string is empty. This works because an innermost pair is adjacent after its contents disappear. Repeated scans and string rebuilding can take O(n²) time and O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A stack keeps unmatched openers in their order of appearance. The newest opener is the only one a closer may match. Remove it only when its type matches, and reject immediately if no matching opener exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read ( then [ in ([{}])\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack contains ( and [\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read { then }\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push { and remove it again\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read ] then )\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack becomes \u003cmark>empty\u003c/mark>, so accept\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After each valid prefix the stack is exactly its unmatched openers in opening order. The next closer must match its top, so rejection is necessary on mismatch. If the scan finishes with an empty stack every opener has a correct closer; otherwise at least one remains open.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def valid_brackets(text):\n    matching = {')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{'}\n    stack = []\n    for char in text:\n        if char in matching:\n            if not stack or stack.pop() != matching[char]:\n                return False\n        else:\n            stack.append(char)\n    return not stack\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each character is pushed or examined once, giving O(n) time. A string of only openers uses O(n) stack space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A closer on an empty stack is invalid, not a reason to pop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Matching totals do not imply correct nesting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Leftover openers invalidate the result; empty input is accepted.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Ignore non-bracket characters before applying the same stack rule.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Report the index of the first invalid closer rather than a Boolean.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:232":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Implement a first-in, first-out queue using two last-in, first-out stacks. Support adding at the back, removing the front, inspecting the front, and checking emptiness. After adding 4 then 7, removing yields 4.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use stack-end operations only. Values may repeat and can be any Python objects. This implementation raises \u003ccode>IndexError\u003c/code> when removing or inspecting an empty queue, instead of inventing a sentinel value. A single operation may take linear time, but the average over a sequence should be constant.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>First-in, first-out behaviour built only from last-in, first-out containers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pouring one stack into another reverses its order. When is it safe to pour?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Queue from two stacks\u003c/p>\u003cp>Push into an in stack; pop and peek from an out stack, refilling out from in only when out is empty. Each element is moved at most once, so every operation is O(1) amortised even though one refill can move n elements.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) amortised per operation, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every removal, move all elements to a second stack, pop the oldest, then move the rest back. Each removal can cost O(n), for O(q²) total time across q operations and O(n) storage. Repeatedly reversing the same elements wastes work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep incoming values in one stack and ready-to-remove values in another. Transfer all incoming values only when the outgoing stack is empty. The transfer reverses arrival order, putting the oldest at the top. New arrivals remain behind all values already in the outgoing stack.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Push 4 then 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>incoming is [4, 7], outgoing is empty\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Peek: transfer 7 then 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>outgoing is [7, 4], top \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Push 9, then pop\u003c/p>\u003cpre>remove \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>; 7 still precedes 9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The queue order is outgoing from top to bottom followed by incoming from bottom to top. Pushing preserves this order. A transfer reverses incoming precisely when there are no older outgoing values, and popping the outgoing top removes exactly the oldest value.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TwoStackQueue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.incoming = []\n        self.outgoing = []\n\n    def push(self, value):\n        self.incoming.append(value)\n\n    def _refill(self):\n        if not self.outgoing:\n            while self.incoming:\n                self.outgoing.append(self.incoming.pop())\n\n    def pop(self):\n        self._refill()\n        return self.outgoing.pop()\n\n    def peek(self):\n        self._refill()\n        return self.outgoing[-1]\n\n    def empty(self):\n        return not self.incoming and not self.outgoing\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each item enters incoming, moves to outgoing at most once, and leaves once. Thus a sequence of q operations costs O(q) amortized time, although an individual refill costs O(n). Space is O(n) for n queued items.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Never transfer incoming onto a nonempty outgoing stack: newer values would jump ahead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Peek must refill just like pop but leave the front in place.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Both stacks must be empty for the queue to be empty.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Build a stack from queues, accepting a costly push or pop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a deque when the exercise does not require stacks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:19":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Remove the node n positions from the end of a singly linked chain and return the resulting head. Positions are one-based: in 4 → 6 → 9 → 2, removing position 2 from the end removes 9. Relink the remaining existing nodes rather than constructing a replacement chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The chain is finite, acyclic, and nonempty; \u003ccode>1 &lt;= n &lt;= length\u003c/code>. The head itself may need removal, so a pointer before the head simplifies the boundary case. A single forward traversal with constant auxiliary space is possible without knowing the length in advance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Delete the node a given distance from the end of a list, in a single pass.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If one pointer starts n + 1 nodes ahead of another and both move together, where is the back pointer when the front one falls off the end?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fast and slow pointers (Floyd)\u003c/p>\u003cp>From a dummy placed before the head, move fast n + 1 steps, then move fast and slow together until fast is None. Slow is now just before the target, so set slow.next = slow.next.next. The dummy makes deleting the head an ordinary case.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First count the nodes, then walk again to the predecessor of the node at index \u003ccode>length - n\u003c/code> and unlink it. This takes O(L) time and O(1) space for length L, but makes two traversals and needs a separate head case unless it uses a sentinel. Alternatively, storing all node references gives random access but costs O(L) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Put a dummy before the head and place \u003ccode>fast\u003c/code> n+1 edges ahead of \u003ccode>slow\u003c/code>, which starts at the dummy. Move both references together. When fast reaches None, slow is immediately before the target, including when that predecessor is the dummy. The fixed gap converts a distance from the end into a forward walk.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Chain 4 -&gt; 6 -&gt; 9 -&gt; 2, n = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slow starts at dummy; move fast three edges to 9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Move both once, then again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>fast reaches None while slow reaches 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Replace slow.next by slow.next.next\u003c/p>\u003cpre>result \u003cmark>4 -&gt; 6 -&gt; 2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Initially the pointers have a gap of n+1 edges. Synchronous movement preserves the gap. At termination exactly n nodes follow slow, so its successor is the nth node from the end. Unlinking that successor preserves the order of every other node; \u003ccode>dummy.next\u003c/code> is the correct new head even when the target was first.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class ListNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = next\n\n\ndef remove_nth_from_end(head, n):\n    dummy = ListNode(0, head)\n    slow = fast = dummy\n    for _ in range(n + 1):\n        fast = fast.next\n    while fast is not None:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next\n    slow.next = slow.next.next\n    return dummy.next\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The fast pointer advances across the chain once, while slow advances at most L times. Total time is O(L), and the dummy and pointers use O(1) auxiliary space. The removed node is not copied, and the original chain is modified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The gap is n+1 when both pointers begin at the dummy; a gap of n puts slow on the target rather than its predecessor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Removing the only node or the original head must return the new \u003ccode>dummy.next\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The stated precondition excludes n=0 and n larger than the length; validate these separately if accepting untrusted input.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the removed node as well as the new head, detaching its next link if needed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Given a known length, make a preliminary count unnecessary and walk directly to the predecessor.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:142":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the actual node at which a singly linked chain first enters a cycle, or None when the chain ends. For A → B → C → D → B, the answer is the node B, not an arbitrary meeting node. Values may repeat, so the answer is an object reference.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every reachable node has one successor or None. The input may be empty, acyclic, a self-loop, or a cycle beginning at the head. The links must remain unchanged, and the method uses O(1) auxiliary references rather than marking nodes or storing a visited set.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return the node where a loop begins, not just whether one exists, with constant memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When fast and slow meet, compare how far each has walked. What does the difference tell you about laps of the cycle?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fast and slow pointers (Floyd)\u003c/p>\u003cp>At the meeting slow has walked t steps and fast 2t, so t is a whole number of laps. Walking mu (the distance from the head to the entry) more steps from the meeting point therefore lands on the entry. Restart one pointer at the head and step both by one: they meet exactly at the entry.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Walk from the head, keeping a set of previously visited node identities. The first repeated identity is the entry because a deterministic successor walk revisits nothing before entering its cycle. This takes O(n) time and O(n) space for n distinct reachable nodes. It is an excellent oracle but does not meet the constant-space goal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First find a meeting using speeds one and two. Let μ be the number of edges from the head to the entry, λ the cycle length, and t the number of slow steps at the meeting. The fast pointer has taken 2t steps, so t is a multiple of λ. Consequently the meeting point is t-μ edges around the cycle from the entry, and advancing μ more edges returns it to the entry. A pointer restarted at the head also reaches the entry after μ steps.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">A -&gt; B -&gt; C -&gt; D -&gt; B\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slow and fast start at A; cycle entry is B\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">After three moves both pointers meet at D\u003c/p>\u003cpre>restart one pointer at A, leave the other at D\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Advance both once: A -&gt; B and D -&gt; B\u003c/p>\u003cpre>their first new meeting is \u003cmark>B\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The initial meeting exists for every cycle because the relative distance changes by one modulo λ. If the fast pointer reaches None there can be no cycle. During the second phase the restarted pointer cannot meet a cycle node before it has traversed its μ-edge tail. At exactly μ steps both pointers are at the entry by the modular argument, proving it is the first meeting.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class ListNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = next\n\n\ndef cycle_entry(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast is not None and fast.next is not None:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n        if slow is fast:\n            seeker = head\n            while seeker is not slow:\n                seeker = seeker.next\n                slow = slow.next\n            return seeker\n    return None\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Finding the meeting and then the entry requires O(n) time over the distinct reachable nodes. There are only a few references, so extra space is O(1). No node is changed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Comparing equal values instead of identical nodes can claim a false meeting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not return the phase-one meeting unless it happens to be the entry.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check the fast pointer's successor before taking two steps; a self-loop and a cycle at the head both work.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Determine only whether a cycle exists and stop at the first meeting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Once the entry is found, walk one lap to obtain the cycle length.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:394":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Expand nested repetition expressions: a positive decimal count immediately followed by brackets repeats the bracket contents that many times. Literal letters are copied. For example \u003ccode>2[a3[b]]\u003c/code> expands to \u003ccode>abbbabbb\u003c/code>. Brackets delimit repetitions and are not included in the output.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assume a well-formed expression with positive counts, lowercase literal letters, balanced brackets, and no other characters; counts can have multiple digits. Nested groups can expand far beyond the encoded input, so any algorithm must at least write the output. The implementation is iterative, avoiding recursion depth limits.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Counts followed by bracketed text that may itself contain counted brackets, such as 2[x3[y]].\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you meet an opening bracket, what must you remember about the text and the count outside it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Stack of open contexts\u003c/p>\u003cp>On \"[\" push (text built so far, repeat count) and start a fresh text; on \"]\" pop and set text = outer + inner * count. Accumulate multi-digit counts with num = num * 10 + d, and collect text in lists joined once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(length of the output) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repeatedly locate an innermost bracketed group, find the digits before it, replace the whole group with the repeated interior, and rescan the resulting string. Although straightforward, repeated searching and copying can cost quadratic time or more in the size of intermediate strings. It also repeatedly examines literal characters already processed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain a list of fragments for the current nesting level. On an opening bracket, save the outer fragments and repetition count, then start a fresh inner list. On a closing bracket, join the inner fragments and append its repeated text to the saved outer list. This stack represents precisely the nesting levels still open.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 2[ then a then 3[\u003c/p>\u003cpre>saved contexts have counts 2 and 3; current text is empty\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read b] and expand the inner context\u003c/p>\u003cpre>outer fragments now represent abbb\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Close the outer context\u003c/p>\u003cpre>repeat abbb twice to produce \u003cmark>abbbabbb\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Inductively, each fragment list is the fully decoded text read so far at its level. Opening a group postpones its contribution until the matching closer. At a closer, the inner list is complete, and repeating it exactly the requested number of times appends the correct contribution to its parent. At the end the only remaining list is the complete output.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def decode_string(text):\n    stack = []\n    parts = []\n    count = 0\n    for char in text:\n        if char.isdigit():\n            count = count * 10 + int(char)\n        elif char == '[':\n            stack.append((parts, count))\n            parts = []\n            count = 0\n        elif char == ']':\n            outer, repeat = stack.pop()\n            outer.append(''.join(parts) * repeat)\n            parts = outer\n        else:\n            parts.append(char)\n    return ''.join(parts)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let N be encoded length and S the sum of lengths of all materialized group expansions, including nested intermediate results and the final output. Time is O(N+S), not necessarily O(N+output length): deeply nested count-one groups can repeatedly copy the same text. With positive counts, retained fragments represent disjoint portions of the final result; peak auxiliary space is O(N+L), where L is final output length, including transient copies.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Accumulate every digit; treating \u003ccode>12\u003c/code> as two counts changes the result.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reset the count after opening a group, and restore the correct outer fragments after closing it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Adjacent groups and literal prefixes or suffixes are valid; malformed input is outside this contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compute decoded length without materializing the string, using a stack of counts and lengths.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stream output with an explicit traversal when the expanded result is too large to retain.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:150":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Evaluate an arithmetic expression supplied in postfix order: each operator follows its two operands. Tokens are signed decimal integers or one of \u003ccode>+\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>-\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>*\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>/\u003c/code>. For tokens \u003ccode>[\"7\", \"3\", \"-\", \"2\", \"*\"]\u003c/code>, the result is 8.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The token sequence is valid, ends with exactly one value, and never divides by zero. Division truncates toward zero, even for negative results. Python's \u003ccode>//\u003c/code> floors instead, and converting a huge integer quotient through a float can lose precision, so use integer arithmetic throughout.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An arithmetic expression in which every operator comes after its two operands.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When an operator arrives, where are its operands, and which of the two is on top?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Expression evaluation with stacks\u003c/p>\u003cp>Push numbers; on an operator pop b (the right operand) and then a (the left) and push a op b. Division truncates towards zero, so use int(a / b) for small values or sign-aware integer division, never Python's //.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repeatedly find an operator immediately preceded by two numeric tokens, replace those three tokens with their evaluated result, and restart the scan. A valid postfix expression always has such a reducible operation until only one value remains. Repeated scanning and list splicing cost O(T²) time and O(T) space for T tokens, ignoring integer bit costs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Scan once while storing values of complete subexpressions. A number is already a complete subexpression. An operator consumes the two most recent subexpressions: pop the right operand first, then the left, and push their result. For division, floor the quotient of absolute values and apply the result's sign.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read 7 and 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack is [7, 3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read -: pop right 3 then left 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push 4; read 2, giving [4, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read *: push 4 * 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>final stack contains \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After each prefix the stack consists of complete subexpression values awaiting future operators, in their original order. Each operator replaces exactly its two operands with their value, maintaining the invariant. A valid full expression has one such value, which is the answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, the stack after processing two independent operands has their left-to-right values, even if either operand was itself produced by many earlier tokens. The stack does not need to retain the original expression text: its future behavior depends only on these computed values. That is why a local reduction is equivalent to evaluating the corresponding subtree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def evaluate_postfix(tokens):\n    stack = []\n    for token in tokens:\n        if token not in {\"+\", \"-\", \"*\", \"/\"}:\n            stack.append(int(token))\n            continue\n        right = stack.pop()\n        left = stack.pop()\n        if token == \"+\":\n            value = left + right\n        elif token == \"-\":\n            value = left - right\n        elif token == \"*\":\n            value = left * right\n        else:\n            value = abs(left) // abs(right)\n            if (left &lt; 0) != (right &lt; 0):\n                value = -value\n        stack.append(value)\n    return stack[-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(T) stack operations and O(T) space in the worst case, under the usual unit-cost arithmetic model. Python integers have arbitrary precision, so arithmetic on large intermediate values has additional bit-dependent time and storage costs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Subtraction and division depend on operand order: the first pop is the right operand.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>-7 // 3\u003c/code> is -3 in Python, whereas truncation toward zero gives -2.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A single numeric token is already the answer; division by zero and malformed token streams are excluded.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Build an expression tree by pushing nodes instead of computed values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add exponentiation with an explicit rule for negative exponents and integer results.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:224":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Evaluate a valid integer expression containing decimal numbers, addition, subtraction, parentheses, unary plus and unary minus. Spaces may appear between tokens. For \u003ccode>8-(3-(2+1))\u003c/code>, the result is 8; for \u003ccode>-(4-7)+2\u003c/code>, it is 5. Do not use Python's expression evaluator.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The expression is nonempty and syntactically valid. Numbers are nonnegative decimal literals; signs belong to operators rather than to the digits. Parentheses may nest, unary signs may repeat, and subtraction is left-associative. Whitespace separates tokens and does not split a number. Only addition and subtraction are present, so a running signed sum is sufficient at each nesting level.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Python integers are arbitrary precision. No artificial overflow or floating-point conversion is required. Very deep nesting is possible, so an explicit stack is safer than recursive descent if interpreter stack depth is limited.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Plus, minus, nested parentheses and minus signs in front of whole groups, to be evaluated without eval.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A parenthesised group is just a sub-result. What do you need to remember about the outside when you step into one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Expression evaluation with stacks\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep result, the sign of the next number and the current digits. On \"(\" push (result, sign) and reset to 0 and +1; on \")\" add the pending number, pop (outer, s) and set result = outer + s * result. A leading or unary minus only sets sign to -1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space for the nesting depth\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find an innermost parenthesized expression, evaluate it by scanning its signed terms, replace that whole region by its integer value, and repeat until no parentheses remain. Repeated searches and string replacements may copy the whole expression once per nesting level, giving O(N²) time in the worst case and O(N) space, excluding integer bit costs. Care is also needed to preserve unary signs when substituting a negative intermediate result.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain a sum for the current level and a sign to apply to the next operand. After a binary operator, set the sign for the next operand; while expecting an operand, multiply the sign by each unary minus, leaving it unchanged for unary plus. When an opening parenthesis appears, save the current sum and pending sign, then start a fresh inner sum. A closing parenthesis turns the inner sum into one operand of its saved outer level.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003ccode>expect_operand\u003c/code> flag distinguishes a binary minus after a value from a unary minus before one. This matters for expressions such as \u003ccode>5--2\u003c/code>, where the two minus signs cancel. The saved sign applies to the whole group, not just its first number.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read -(4-7)+2: the leading minus sets sign to -1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>opening parenthesis saves outer sum 0 and sign -1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Inside the group, add 4 and subtract 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>inner sum is -3; close and restore 0 + (-1)*(-3) = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read +2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>final sum is \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For each active level, \u003ccode>total\u003c/code> equals the sum of all completed operands at that level, and \u003ccode>sign\u003c/code> is the accumulated sign for the next operand. Number tokens complete one operand, while opening a group saves the unfinished outer context. By induction on nesting depth, closing a group yields its correct value, which is incorporated using the saved sign. The top-level sum therefore equals the expression once every token is processed. Validity guarantees that each closer has a matching saved context and that the scan ends after an operand.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Repeated unary signs change only the pending sign; they do not finish an operand or alter the accumulated total. A closing parenthesis does finish an operand in the outer context, so a following minus is binary. These transitions make nested negative groups and adjacent operator signs behave consistently without inserting artificial zero literals into the expression.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def calculate(text):\n    total = 0\n    sign = 1\n    expect_operand = True\n    stack = []\n    i = 0\n    while i &lt; len(text):\n        char = text[i]\n        if char.isspace():\n            i += 1\n        elif char.isdigit():\n            value = 0\n            while i &lt; len(text) and text[i].isdigit():\n                value = value * 10 + int(text[i])\n                i += 1\n            total += sign * value\n            sign = 1\n            expect_operand = False\n        elif char == '(':\n            stack.append((total, sign))\n            total, sign, expect_operand = 0, 1, True\n            i += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            outer, multiplier = stack.pop()\n            total = outer + multiplier * total\n            sign, expect_operand = 1, False\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            factor = 1 if char == '+' else -1\n            sign = sign * factor if expect_operand else factor\n            expect_operand = True\n            i += 1\n    return total\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each character is consumed once, so there are O(N) parsing operations. The context stack uses O(D) entries for maximum nesting depth D, at most O(N). These are unit-cost arithmetic bounds; building and operating on arbitrary-size integers also has bit-dependent costs. The input text is unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Applying a group's outer minus only to its first term gives the wrong answer for \u003ccode>-(4-7)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treating every minus as binary fails on a leading minus, repeated signs, or signs following an opening parenthesis.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not use \u003ccode>eval\u003c/code>, floor division, or floating-point arithmetic; they are unnecessary here.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty parentheses, missing operands, and invalid characters are outside the contract; validate them separately for untrusted input.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add multiplication and division by tracking precedence or using a separate operator stack.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Produce an abstract syntax tree instead of immediately evaluating each group.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:287":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An array has n+1 entries, all integers from 1 through n. Exactly one distinct value appears more than once, possibly more than twice. Find that repeated value without modifying the array. For \u003ccode>[2, 1, 3, 2]\u003c/code>, return 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assume n is at least one, the value range and unique repeated value are guaranteed, and random array indexing is available. The desired extra space is O(1). Since the array has more entries than possible values, a repetition must exist; however, sorting it in place would violate the read-only requirement.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>n + 1 values in the range 1..n with one value repeated; the array must not change and extra space must be O(1).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Read nums[i] as \"the node after i\". Starting from index 0, what shape does following those links trace out?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fast and slow pointers (Floyd)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Index 0 is never a value, so following i -&gt; nums[i] from 0 walks a tail into a cycle. The cycle's entry is reached by two different indices, so it is the repeated value. Run both phases of Floyd's algorithm on indices.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compare every pair of entries and return the first equal value. Under the uniqueness promise this is the desired duplicate. It takes O(n²) time and O(1) space without mutating the array. A set makes the scan linear but requires O(n) space; sorting a copy also consumes linear extra space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Treat index i as a node whose successor is \u003ccode>nums[i]\u003c/code>. Following successors from index 0 must eventually repeat, because every successor is an index in 1..n. Index 0 is never a successor, so the walk has a nonempty tail before entering the cycle. The entry has two different incoming edges along this walk: one from the tail and one from the cycle. Their source indices contain the same value, which is the unique duplicate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Find a meeting by moving one index once and another twice. Then restart one index at 0 and move both one step at a time; their first meeting is the cycle entry.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Array [2, 1, 3, 2] gives edges 0 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>the path enters its loop at index 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Slow and fast meet at index 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>restart slow at 0; leave fast at 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Advance each once: 0 -&gt; 2 and 3 -&gt; 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return the entry value \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Let μ be the tail length and λ the cycle length. At the first meeting after t slow steps, the two paths differ by t steps, a multiple of λ. The meeting point is therefore μ steps behind the entry modulo λ. Advancing both the restarted pointer and the meeting pointer by μ steps puts them at the entry; they cannot meet earlier because the restarted pointer remains outside the cycle. By the incoming-edge argument, that entry is the repeated value.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def find_duplicate(nums):\n    slow = fast = 0\n    while True:\n        slow = nums[slow]\n        fast = nums[nums[fast]]\n        if slow == fast:\n            break\n    slow = 0\n    while slow != fast:\n        slow = nums[slow]\n        fast = nums[fast]\n    return slow\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The functional graph has n+1 indices. Each phase takes O(n) steps, so total time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1). No entry is written. The validity guarantees are essential for safe indexing and for interpreting the entry as the sole repeated value.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start from index 0; starting from an arbitrary value changes the tail argument and can produce the wrong entry.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the entry index itself, not \u003ccode>nums[entry]\u003c/code>, which may be different.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A duplicate can occur more than twice; n=1 produces \u003ccode>[1, 1]\u003c/code> and a self-loop at index 1.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Binary-search the value range using counts at or below each midpoint, giving O(n log n) time and O(1) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If mutation is allowed, rearrange or mark entries to expose a repeated value, with a different input contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:25":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reverse a finite singly linked chain in consecutive groups of k nodes. Keep the final group unchanged if it has fewer than k nodes. Return the new head and reuse the original nodes. For 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 with k=2, return 2 → 1 → 4 → 3 → 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input is acyclic, may be empty, and k is a positive integer. Values can repeat; correctness concerns node identity and link order, not just values. The nodes must be relinked in place with O(1) extra references. A dummy before the original head avoids a special case for the first reversed group.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The final short group must stay in its original order, so the algorithm must check that k nodes exist before changing any of their links. If k exceeds the entire chain length, return the original head unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reverse every consecutive block of k nodes, leaving a final short block as it is.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Before reversing a block, make sure it really has k nodes. Which links must you reconnect after reversing it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Pointer rewiring with a dummy head\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep group_prev, the node before the block (a dummy at first). Find the k-th node; if it is missing, stop. Reverse the block so its first node points to the node after the block, then set group_prev.next to the old k-th node and move group_prev to the old first node, now the block's tail.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Read all nodes into an array, reverse each complete slice of k references, and reconnect the array in order. This takes O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space for n nodes. Reversing values rather than nodes might be shorter but changes the association of values to node identities and is not the requested operation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Another constant-space approach can repeatedly locate each group's end from the start, then perform pointer rewiring. The key is to avoid scanning from the global head for every group, which could cost O(n²); advancing a boundary pointer after each completed group keeps the scans local.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain \u003ccode>group_prev\u003c/code>, the node immediately before the untouched suffix. Walk k links to find the last node of the next full group. If it does not exist, stop without modifying that suffix. Otherwise save \u003ccode>group_next\u003c/code>, the first node beyond the group, and reverse the group's arrows, initially pointing the old first node to \u003ccode>group_next\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>After reversal, connect \u003ccode>group_prev.next\u003c/code> to the old kth node. The old first node has become the group's tail, so it is the next \u003ccode>group_prev\u003c/code>. Both boundary links are thus correct before the next iteration starts.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Dummy -&gt; 1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5, k = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>locate 2 and save successor 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse 1 and 2, reconnect the dummy\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix is \u003cmark>2 -&gt; 1\u003c/mark>, group_prev is 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reverse 3 and 4, reconnect after 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix is \u003cmark>2 -&gt; 1 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Only node 5 remains, fewer than k\u003c/p>\u003cpre>leave it unchanged and return \u003cmark>2 -&gt; 1 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>At the start of each iteration, the prefix through \u003ccode>group_prev\u003c/code> is correctly transformed and the remaining suffix retains its original order. Checking the kth node proves the next k nodes exist. Reversing exactly those nodes with initial predecessor \u003ccode>group_next\u003c/code> produces the reverse order and connects its tail to the untouched remainder. Reconnecting the prefix establishes the invariant for the next group. If fewer than k nodes remain, the invariant and stop rule give the required final suffix.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Within a full group, \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> heads the already reversed portion, ending at the saved successor, while \u003ccode>curr\u003c/code> begins the part not yet reversed. Saving \u003ccode>following\u003c/code> before changing a link keeps that unprocessed part reachable. Each iteration moves one node across the boundary; after k moves, the old kth node heads the whole reversed group and every original node remains present.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class ListNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, next=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = next\n\n\ndef reverse_k_group(head, k):\n    if k &lt;= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"k must be positive\")\n    dummy = ListNode(0, head)\n    group_prev = dummy\n    while True:\n        kth = group_prev\n        for _ in range(k):\n            kth = kth.next\n            if kth is None:\n                return dummy.next\n        group_next = kth.next\n        prev, curr = group_next, group_prev.next\n        while curr is not group_next:\n            following = curr.next\n            curr.next = prev\n            prev, curr = curr, following\n        old_first = group_prev.next\n        group_prev.next = kth\n        group_prev = old_first\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Finding each group end scans each full group once, and reversing scans those nodes once more. The final failed scan visits fewer than k nodes, so total time is O(n), including k larger than n. Only a fixed number of node references and one dummy are used, giving O(1) auxiliary space. Links in complete groups are changed in place.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do not start reversing until the kth node is confirmed; otherwise a short suffix is accidentally reversed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Save the successor beyond the group before modifying links, or the remainder can become unreachable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Advance \u003ccode>group_prev\u003c/code> to the old first node, now the tail, not to the new group head.\u003c/li>\u003cli>k=1 preserves the chain; empty input and k greater than its length return the original head. Nonpositive k raises \u003ccode>ValueError\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse alternating full groups while leaving intervening groups untouched.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reverse the final short group too by changing the incomplete-group policy.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
